<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Title>Quartz.NET Jobs</Title>
    <Description>Quartz.NET Jobs - the jobs that ship ready to schedule: directory and file scanning, sending mail, and running a native process; $(Description)</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(PackageTags);directory-scan;file-watcher;send-mail;native-job</PackageTags>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Quartz</RootNamespace>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>True</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <!--
      The claim, and what it is worth - the same statement Quartz.csproj makes, and read the same way.
      IsAotCompatible turns on IsTrimmable and the trim, AOT and single-file analyzers, all of which were
      already on here (issue #3431); what it adds is the statement, so the four are spelled out beside it.

      What is proven: Quartz.Trimming.Canary references this package, is published by ILCompiler as a native
      executable on Windows, Linux and macOS on every pull request, and is run. It schedules a
      DirectoryScanJob configured through DirectoryScanOptions, which scans a directory, finds the listener
      its job data names through the container by the name of its type, and tells it about the file it found.

      What a native publish reports against this package: nothing. One IL2026 was recorded when the analyzers
      were first turned on here (issue #3431) - DirectoryScanJob found its listener by sweeping every loaded
      assembly for a type of that name - and the sweep went away when the job was made to look only where
      the application put something: a keyed registration, a registered listener, the scheduler context.
      The baseline files outlived the warning they recorded until the canary's publish showed nothing for
      them to record, so there is no TrimAnalysisBaseline.cs beside this file any more, and a warning
      appearing here is a new one - fix it rather than starting a baseline for it; build/Build.cs fails the
      canary leg on one. Nothing here reflects: the jobs are ordinary types the container constructs.
      NativeJob and SendMailJob are not run by the canary, since one starts a process and the other needs a
      mail server; neither reflects either.
    -->
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
    <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableAotAnalyzer>true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
    <EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>true</EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz\Quartz.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>